# Editor / Dev-Only Systems

Components that only exist in the editor or development builds — dev overlays, debug HUDs, event logs, profiler widgets, in-scene test helpers — are typically guarded by `#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD`. Such a type **does not exist in a release player**, so it must **never** be serialized onto a prefab or scene that ships in a build.

## Why a serialized reference breaks the release build

When a release build loads a scene/prefab that references a stripped-out type, Unity cannot resolve the script and logs:

```
The referenced script (Your.Namespace.DevOverlay) on this Behaviour is missing!
A scripted object (probably Your.Namespace.DevOverlay?) has a different serialization
layout when loading. (Read 32 bytes but expected 48 bytes)
```

These are shipped-to-players errors that never appear in the editor (where the type compiles), so they slip through until someone opens the release console.

## The pattern: self-spawn under the guard

Guard the whole file with the `#if`, and have the component create itself at runtime via `[RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod]`. Author any UI/state in code (or load authored data by address at runtime) so nothing needs to be serialized on an asset.

```csharp
#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class DevOverlay : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Self-spawns instead of being placed on a prefab/scene: this type is compiled out of
    // release builds, so a serialized reference to it would become a "missing script" +
    // serialization-layout error in the player. Idempotent — bails if one already exists.
    [RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod(RuntimeInitializeLoadType.AfterSceneLoad)]
    private static void EnsureInstance()
    {
        if (FindAnyObjectByType<DevOverlay>(FindObjectsInactive.Include) != null)
        {
            return;
        }

        new GameObject(nameof(DevOverlay)).AddComponent<DevOverlay>();
    }
}
#endif
```

- Make the bootstrap **idempotent** (`HasInstance` / `FindAnyObjectByType` guard) — `AfterSceneLoad` can fire on every scene load.
- A dev-only *helper* that rides on another dev system can instead be `AddComponent`ed from that system's guarded bootstrap.
- Wire providers/config the same way the rest of the project wires runtime bootstrap — behind the `#if`, not via inspector fields on a shipped asset.

## Do not

- Place a dev-guarded `MonoBehaviour` on a prefab or in a scene that ships (even "just for testing" — it gets committed and ships).
- Rely on `[SerializeField]` inspector data for a dev-only component; provide sensible code defaults so a code-spawned instance is fully functional.
- Leave an orphan prefab/scene whose only purpose was to carry a now-self-spawning dev component — delete it.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD` MonoBehaviour serialized on a shipped prefab/scene
- [ ] Dev-only systems self-spawn via `[RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod]`, guarded and idempotent
- [ ] Dev components work from code defaults, not inspector-serialized data absent in release
- [ ] Prefabs/scenes that existed only to host such a component are removed
